{0 Describing a world}

The engine has two ways in, and this is the newer one. {b Making a game on
CamlCast} is the other: it opens a window, builds a {!Camlcast_core.World}, and
drives it with an [update] and a [view]. Everything it teaches still works and
still ships.

This page is about the layer over the top, where a game says what its world
{e should be} — every frame, from nothing — and the runtime works out what
changed. It is React's idea, in OCaml, over a raycaster.

{1 One library}

{[
  open Camlcast
]}

That is all of it. What is in {!Camlcast} is what a description is made of; what
is not in it is the platform underneath — [Engine], [Renderer], [Framebuffer],
[World], [Player]. Those are reachable by adding [camlcast.core] to a dune file
and saying so, which is the boundary being a decision with a diff rather than
something autocomplete finds for you.

{1 The smallest world}

{[
  let level =
    P.(
      world ~atmosphere:Atmosphere.default ~spawn:("room", Vec.make (-4.5) 0.)
        [
          room ~name:"room"
            ~floor:(floor ~plane:ground ~material:stone)
            ~ceiling:(roof ~plane:(Plane.above ground 4.) ~material:stone)
            [ outline ~height:4. ~material:stone corners ];
        ])

  let () = ignore (Run.play ~title:"A room" level)
]}

{!Camlcast.P} holds the parts, and is written round a description rather than
opened over a file. Its names are short and ordinary — [wall], [room], [text],
[path] — so a local open puts them exactly where a world is being written and
nowhere else, and marks where that starts and stops.

[examples/described_room.ml] is that program, compiled with this repository so
it cannot drift from the engine. [examples/room.ml] beside it is the same room
the older way, and [test_stage] renders both and compares every pixel.

{1 Winding is not your problem}

A room is black from the inside if its boundary is wound the wrong way round.
Nothing catches it, nothing wants the reversed version, and the symptom points
nowhere near the cause.

{!Camlcast.P.outline} settles it by not asking: it measures the loop it was
given and winds it correctly, so the same corners in either order build the same
room. {!Camlcast.P.path} — an open run, for a boundary with a {!Camlcast.P.doorway}
in it — measures the loop it {e would} be if its ends were joined, which is the
same measurement.

A free-standing {!Camlcast.P.wall} needs no rule: it is drawn from both sides
and has no inside for a normal to face into.

{1 Components}

A component is a function from props to a description. It is declared once, at
the top level:

{[
  let torch =
    Element.declare ~name:"torch" @@ fun pos ->
    let lit, set_lit = Hook.use_state true in
    Events.use_key_down Key.e (fun () -> set_lit (not lit));
    P.sprite ~size:0.8 ~image:(if lit then flame else stub) pos
]}

{b At the top level} is a rule and not a style. A component is identified by its
[render] being physically the same closure, because a function is the only thing
a component is — so one built inside another render is a fresh closure every
frame, is never the same component as last frame, and is torn down and rebuilt
along with everything under it. It will look as though state does not work.
{!Camlcast.Element.declare} exists partly to make the right thing the easy one.

Anything that can be rearranged wants a [key]. Among a parent's children, a
keyed element is matched to last frame's by its key wherever it has moved to,
and an unkeyed one by its position — so a list of sprites that sorts itself
keeps each sprite's state only if the sprites are keyed.

{1 State, and when it shows}

{!Camlcast.Hook} is [use_state], [use_ref], [use_memo] and [use_effect]. The one
rule is that hooks are called unconditionally, in the same order, every render:
nothing names them, so their order is their identity. Where React's docs ask
this and shrug, this raises — see {!Camlcast.Hook} for exactly what it catches
and the one thing it cannot.

A setter writes its slot and marks the tree. It does {e not} re-enter the render
that is running: the change shows up next frame. At sixty frames a second that
is sixteen milliseconds, and in exchange no render can loop.

{1 Time, input, and being looked at}

{!Camlcast.Events} is what a component can know about the frame it is being
rendered for. [use_frame] runs after every frame with its length; [use_key_down]
runs on the tap; [use_crossed] runs once for each doorway the frame went
through. None of them run {e during} a render, because a render is meant to be a
pure function of props and state.

{!Camlcast.P.wall}, {!Camlcast.P.sprite} and {!Camlcast.P.doorway} take
[on_gaze] and [on_use], which are the three things an eye can stop on.
[on_gaze] is an enter and a leave, not a poll. [on_use] is handed an
{!Camlcast.Aim.spot} saying where on the thing the crosshair was — which is what
marking a wall where you pointed needs.

{1 Composing}

A higher-order component takes children and puts something around them. There is
no machinery to it, because a description is data and a component is a function:

{[
  let lit_world =
    Element.declare ~name:"lit_world" @@ fun props ->
    P.world ~atmosphere:(air ~light:props.light) ~spawn:props.spawn
      props.children
]}

[examples/described_fuse.ml] is a whole small game built that way, and
[examples/game.ml] beside it is the same game the older way. Reading the two
together is the shortest account of what this layer is for: [game.ml] keeps one
record of the entire world — a phase, a clock and a player — advanced by one
[update], because that is the only shape the old API has. In the described
version the phase and the clock belong to the component that uses them, the
player belongs to the runtime, and there is nowhere all three are written down
together.

{1 Before anyone walks into it}

{!Camlcast.Check.report} reads a description for what a compiler cannot see: two
doorways joined to the same third, a room nothing leads to, a spawn inside a
wall, a step in the floor where two rooms meet. It is a pure function of a
description, so the natural home is a test — one case per level.

Where it can, a diagnostic names the component that wrote the offending part
rather than the room the engine found it in.

Pressing {b F3} in a running game draws the room from above: every wall with a
short tick pointing the way its normal faces, doorways green when linked and red
when they lead nowhere, and a mark on anything {!Camlcast.Check.world} has
something to say about. It is the fastest way to see a boundary wound the wrong
way round.

{1 What it costs}

Assembling a whole world every frame sounds like something to design around. It
is not: describing five rooms and a hundred and forty-five walls — the size of
the largest world this engine has — takes about twenty microseconds against the
fourteen milliseconds the renderer spends drawing that frame. [bench/frame.exe]
is where that was measured and is there to be re-run rather than re-argued.
*)
